  "id": "Compound::DB00816 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Orciprenaline, a member of the adrenergic beta2-agonist class, is strongly linked to the receptor Adrb2 through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Orciprenaline, as an adrenergic beta2-agonist, binds to Adrb2, with the lowest common ancestor of Orciprenaline and its downstream targets being the organic cyclic compound. This indicates a shared biological pathway involving adrenergic signaling. The presence of multiple adrenergic beta2-agonists, including Pirbuterol, Formoterol, and Arformoterol, all of which bind to Adrb2, further supports the notion that Orciprenaline exerts its effects through a similar mechanism. The integrated evidence suggests that Orciprenaline acts as a modulator of Adrb2 through a common pathway, likely involving the activation of adrenergic receptors and subsequent downstream signaling. The high scores in the paths indicate a strong, biologically plausible relationship, supported by the shared classification and binding evidence.",
